---
/**
 * The device frame - bezel, screen mask, and the container everything inside sizes
 * against. `design-system.md` §6 gives this component sole ownership of the shell so a
 * screen never draws its own bezel and the two can never drift apart.
 *
 * The whole device is `aria-hidden`. It is decorative UI: the copy beside it carries the
 * meaning, and §6 is explicit that a screen reader must never have to walk a fake app.
 * Nothing inside is focusable, so it is out of the tab order by construction rather than
 * by `tabindex` housekeeping.
 *
 * The screen sets `container-type: size`, which is what lets the screens inside express
 * every dimension as a fraction of the screen the way the SwiftUI original does
 * (`min(18, H * 0.022)` becomes `min(18px, 2.2cqh)`). Change the device's width and every
 * proportion inside follows, exactly as it does when the app runs on a bigger handset.
 */
---

<div class="device" aria-hidden="true" data-device>
  <div class="shade"></div>
  <div class="bezel">
    <div class="screen">
      <slot />
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .device {
    /* 390x844 - the iPhone the whole site is designed against (design-system.md §4). */
    aspect-ratio: 390 / 844;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    position: relative;
  }

  /*
   * The shell's shadow, on its own layer so it can go out with the lights.
   *
   * It cannot live on `.bezel`: that element holds the screen, so fading it would fade
   * the feed with it - and animating `box-shadow` is a repaint per frame, which this
   * repo bans in anything scrubbed. An `opacity` on a layer that draws nothing else is
   * composited. `--blackout` is 0 on any page that does not drive it, so the shadow is
   * simply present everywhere else, `/kit` included.
   *
   * Floored at 0.3 from Gate C rev 16 to rev 19 (owner: "add back the shadow on the
   * phone"). Rev 20 (owner): the shadow should go fully out with the background once it
   * reaches true full black, not stay at a floor - `--blackout` itself no longer reverses
   * (rev 19), so there is nothing left for a floor to protect against.
   */
  .shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border-radius: 13.6%/6.3%;
    box-shadow:
      0 2px 1px rgba(250, 250, 250, 0.08) inset,
      0 40px 80px -20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
    opacity: calc(1 - var(--blackout));
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .bezel {
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: 13.6%/6.3%;
    padding: 1.9%;
    /* Transparent (owner, Gate C rev 13). The shell is read entirely from its shadow -
     * which `.shade` owns - and the screen it frames, so the page's own ground shows
     * through the bezel instead of a second, darker slab sitting on top of it. */
    background: none;
  }

  .screen {
    height: 100%;
    /* Concentric with the bezel: outer radius minus the bezel's own thickness. */
    border-radius: 11.7%/5.4%;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The classic iOS Safari mockup bug is a transformed child bleeding past a rounded
     * clip. A new stacking context is what actually holds the corners. */
    isolation: isolate;
    position: relative;
    container-type: size;
    background: var(--color-ink-950);
  }
</style>
